+## L16: `HGO-GIT` ETIMEDOUT in a PreToolUse hook
+
+### Facts
+
+- The code is `HGO-GIT`, thrown by the one helper `git(root, args, spawn)` in `PC/lib/human-guard-override.mjs:204-215`: fixed `timeout: 5000`, `shell: false`, one attempt, any non-zero status or spawn error throws `repository identity is unavailable (operation=rev-parse---show-toplevel, outcome=ETIMEDOUT)`. `outcome` is `result.error.code` first (:210-211), which is how `ETIMEDOUT` appears. There is no retry: a search of the file and of `PC/lib/git-cmd.mjs` finds none.
+- `topology()` (:224-229) is the first call of the override planning and runs `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (:226). The helper serves `topology`, `repositoryObservation` (:988-995), the cross-repository probe (:1303) and the reconcile window (:6857-6913, including `write-tree`, an idempotent content-addressed object write). Two more spawns carry the same 5000 ms outside the helper (:965, :967) and one advisory (:1092).
+- **Why it hurts.** In the Antigravity and Codex pre-tool hooks the planning failure is classified as a host boundary and turned into `external-operator-required` / `HGO-EXTERNAL-REPOSITORY-OBSERVATION` (`PC/hooks/antigravity-pretool-guard.mjs:1219-1244`, same in `codex-pretool-guard.mjs:661`): a transient timeout under parallel load becomes a hand-over to the human, with no retry route. The existing tests pin `HGO-GIT` for `EPERM` and for a genuine failure (`human-guard-override.test.mjs:4748,5443,5633`), not for a timeout.
+- **Hook budget.** Most hook entries in `PC/hooks/hooks.json` carry `timeout: 10` seconds (e.g. :23, :45, :56); which entry covers the planning path and the Antigravity host's own hook limit were not traced. NOT MEASURED: what the host does when a hook overruns (the denial text may be lost), so the budget must not be exceeded.
+- **One planning makes several git calls.** `topology()` runs two (:226,:228); `repositoryObservation` runs four more through the helper (:988-995) and two outside it (:965,:967). A retry per call therefore cannot keep one planning under 10 s.
+
+### What would be safe
+
+A retry bounded per PLANNING, not per call: the helper keeps the first attempt at 5000 ms (so every other caller of the shared helper is unchanged) and retries once, only when `result.error?.code === "ETIMEDOUT"` (never on a non-zero exit status), and only while a deadline of about 9 s since the planning's first git call leaves at least 2 s, with the remaining time as the retry's timeout. The retried calls are read-only or idempotent (`write-tree` is content-addressed). On the final timeout keep today's `HGO-GIT` text and append the runnable step "retry the identical action once; if it repeats, run `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` in the project root and look at the machine load". No time-based expiry of any artifact, no signature or approval touched. The deadline needs a start carried by `topology()` (a design choice the dispatch makes, not this recon); the two spawns at :965 and :967 could route through the helper (optional rider).
+
+**Failing-first proof:** the helper takes an injected `spawn` and clock. Case 1: first call `{ error: { code: "ETIMEDOUT" } }`, second `{ status: 0, stdout }` returns the value (today it throws `HGO-GIT`). Case 2: two timeouts throw `HGO-GIT` with `outcome=ETIMEDOUT` and the spawn ran exactly twice. Case 3: `{ status: 128 }` is NOT retried (spawn ran once). Case 4: with the clock past the deadline, no retry. **Protected file:** `human-guard-override.mjs` is a guard library; whether it is on the shipped protected baseline was not checked (INFERRED yes), so the held patch is applied through the protected-source route.
